## 1. Factory Method

### What it is

Defines an interface for creating an object, but lets subclasses (or implementors) decide which class to instantiate. The creator depends on an abstraction, not a concrete type.

### Applicability

| Context | Relevance | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Java 21 standalone | **Moderate** | Sealed classes + switch expressions cover many cases more concisely. Still essential for open/extensible hierarchies. |
| Java 21 + Spring Boot | **High** | Every `@Bean` method is a factory method. Framework internals are built on it. |

### Practical Examples

**Java 21 — sealed types make a factory method concise:**

```java
sealed interface Notification permits EmailNotification, SmsNotification, PushNotification {}

record EmailNotification(String to, String subject) implements Notification {}
record SmsNotification(String phone, String message) implements Notification {}
record PushNotification(String deviceToken, String payload) implements Notification {}

// Factory method — dispatch by type
public static Notification create(String channel, Map<String, String> params) {
    return switch (channel) {
        case "email" -> new EmailNotification(params.get("to"), params.get("subject"));
        case "sms"   -> new SmsNotification(params.get("phone"), params.get("message"));
        case "push"  -> new PushNotification(params.get("token"), params.get("payload"));
        default      -> throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown channel: " + channel);
    };
}
```

**Spring Boot — `@Bean` as factory method:**

```java
@Configuration
public class StorageConfig {

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnProperty(name = "storage.type", havingValue = "s3")
    public StorageService s3StorageService(S3Client client) {
        return new S3StorageService(client);
    }

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnProperty(name = "storage.type", havingValue = "local", matchIfMissing = true)
    public StorageService localStorageService(@Value("${storage.path}") String path) {
        return new LocalStorageService(path);
    }
}
```

The caller (`StorageService` injection point) never knows which concrete class it receives.

### When it is the Right Choice

- You want to decouple object creation from business logic.
- The concrete type is determined at runtime (config, environment, user input).
- You are building an extensible library or framework where consumers plug in their own types.
- Multiple products share a common interface and you want to swap them transparently.

### When it is the Wrong Choice

- The type hierarchy is closed and small — a simple `switch` or `if` is clearer and has no extra indirection.
- You need the pattern only for a single concrete class — this is over-engineering.
- In Spring Boot, reaching for a hand-rolled factory when `@ConditionalOn*` already handles the dispatch.

### What You Achieve When Used Well

- Clean inversion of control: business logic talks to interfaces, never to `new ConcreteClass()`.
- Easy extensibility — add a new product type without touching the creator.
- Testability: inject a mock factory in tests, get predictable products.

### Disasters When Used Wrong

- **Factory explosion**: a separate factory class per domain object, each with one method, for no reason. You end up maintaining twice the classes with zero benefit.
- **Leaky abstractions**: the factory method returns an interface but the caller immediately casts to a concrete type — defeating the entire point.
- **Hidden coupling**: factory logic hard-codes every concrete type in a long `if-else` chain. The factory becomes a "god switch" that must change every time a new type is added, violating the Open/Closed Principle.

---

## 2. Abstract Factory

### What it is

Provides an interface for creating **families** of related objects without specifying their concrete classes. Where Factory Method creates one product, Abstract Factory creates a coordinated set of products.

### Applicability

| Context | Relevance | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Java 21 standalone | **Moderate** | Useful for platform/theme families. Sealed interfaces reduce the verbosity of concrete factories. |
| Java 21 + Spring Boot | **Moderate** | Mostly consumed, not written. Spring auto-configuration wires product families. Explicit use in multi-tenant or multi-environment setups. |

### Practical Examples

**Java 21 — a notification channel family:**

```java
// Product interfaces
interface MessageSender   { void send(String to, String body); }
interface DeliveryTracker { Status track(String messageId); }

// Abstract factory
interface NotificationChannelFactory {
    MessageSender   createSender();
    DeliveryTracker createTracker();
}

// Concrete factory: email family
class EmailChannelFactory implements NotificationChannelFactory {
    public MessageSender   createSender()  { return new SmtpSender(); }
    public DeliveryTracker createTracker() { return new EmailDeliveryTracker(); }
}

// Concrete factory: SMS family
class SmsChannelFactory implements NotificationChannelFactory {
    public MessageSender   createSender()  { return new TwilioSender(); }
    public DeliveryTracker createTracker() { return new SmsDeliveryTracker(); }
}
```

**Spring Boot — auto-configuration as Abstract Factory:**

```java
// Spring wires a whole datasource family per profile
@Configuration
@Profile("postgres")
public class PostgresDataConfig {
    @Bean public DataSource dataSource() { /* postgres */ }
    @Bean public TransactionManager transactionManager(DataSource ds) { /* postgres tx */ }
    @Bean public JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate(DataSource ds) { return new JdbcTemplate(ds); }
}

@Configuration
@Profile("h2")
public class H2DataConfig {
    @Bean public DataSource dataSource() { /* in-memory H2 */ }
    @Bean public TransactionManager transactionManager(DataSource ds) { /* H2 tx */ }
    @Bean public JdbcTemplate jdbcTemplate(DataSource ds) { return new JdbcTemplate(ds); }
}
```

Switching profiles swaps the entire family atomically.

### When it is the Right Choice

- Products come in **families** that must be used together consistently (e.g., a sender and its tracker must both be the SMS variants or both the email variants — mixing breaks correctness).
- You need to switch entire environments or themes at a single configuration point.
- You are building a cross-platform toolkit (UI widgets, persistence adapters, cloud provider clients).

### When it is the Wrong Choice

- Products are independent — there is no consistency constraint between them. Just use separate Factory Methods.
- You only have one concrete family now and "might add more later" — YAGNI applies. Add the abstraction when the second family actually arrives.
- The team is small and the domain is simple. The class count (two interfaces plus N×M implementations) outweighs the benefit.

### What You Achieve When Used Well

- **Family consistency guaranteed at compile time** — you cannot mix an `SmtpSender` with an `SmsDeliveryTracker` accidentally.
- Entire environments (test, staging, prod) can be swapped by changing one factory binding.
- Adding a new family (e.g., WhatsApp channel) requires only a new concrete factory class — existing code is untouched.

### Disasters When Used Wrong

- **Interface bloat**: adding a third product to the family requires changing the abstract factory interface and all concrete factories — a shotgun change across the codebase.
- **Premature abstraction**: building the full factory hierarchy for a system that currently has exactly one environment, and may never have a second. You pay the complexity tax indefinitely.
- **Invisible coupling**: two teams each independently add a product to the family interface, causing merge conflicts and forcing all factories to implement products they don't logically own.

---

## 3. Builder

### What it is

Separates the construction of a complex object from its representation, so the same construction process can produce different representations. In practice: a fluent API for building objects with many optional parameters.

### Applicability

| Context | Relevance | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Java 21 standalone | **High** | Records handle tiny immutable value objects. Builder remains essential for objects with many optional fields, validation logic, or multi-step construction. |
| Java 21 + Spring Boot | **High** | You consume Spring's own builders constantly (`WebClient.builder()`, `HttpSecurity`, `UriComponentsBuilder`). You write builders for DTOs, domain aggregates, test fixtures. |

### Practical Examples

**Java 21 — when a record is enough (simple case):**

```java
// Don't build a builder for this — a record is better
record Point(double x, double y) {}

var p = new Point(3.0, 4.0); // clear, immutable, no boilerplate
```

**Java 21 — when a builder is the right tool:**

```java
public final class HttpRequest {
    private final URI uri;
    private final String method;
    private final Map<String, String> headers;
    private final String body;
    private final Duration timeout;
    private final int retries;

    private HttpRequest(Builder b) {
        this.uri     = Objects.requireNonNull(b.uri, "uri required");
        this.method  = b.method;
        this.headers = Map.copyOf(b.headers);
        this.body    = b.body;
        this.timeout = b.timeout;
        this.retries = b.retries;
    }

    public static Builder builder(URI uri) { return new Builder(uri); }

    public static final class Builder {
        private final URI uri;
        private String method = "GET";
        private Map<String, String> headers = new HashMap<>();
        private String body;
        private Duration timeout = Duration.ofSeconds(30);
        private int retries = 0;

        private Builder(URI uri) { this.uri = uri; }

        public Builder method(String m)            { this.method = m;   return this; }
        public Builder header(String k, String v)  { headers.put(k, v); return this; }
        public Builder body(String b)              { this.body = b;      return this; }
        public Builder timeout(Duration d)         { this.timeout = d;   return this; }
        public Builder retries(int r)              { this.retries = r;   return this; }
        public HttpRequest build()                 { return new HttpRequest(this); }
    }
}

// Usage — reads like a sentence
var req = HttpRequest.builder(URI.create("https://api.example.com/orders"))
    .method("POST")
    .header("Content-Type", "application/json")
    .header("Authorization", "Bearer " + token)
    .body(json)
    .timeout(Duration.ofSeconds(10))
    .retries(3)
    .build();
```

**Spring Boot — consuming the framework's builders:**

```java
// WebClient
WebClient client = WebClient.builder()
    .baseUrl("https://api.example.com")
    .defaultHeader(HttpHeaders.CONTENT_TYPE, MediaType.APPLICATION_JSON_VALUE)
    .filter(ExchangeFilterFunctions.basicAuthentication(user, pass))
    .build();

// Spring Security
@Bean
public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
    return http
        .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
            .requestMatchers("/public/**").permitAll()
            .anyRequest().authenticated())
        .sessionManagement(s -> s.sessionCreationPolicy(STATELESS))
        .oauth2ResourceServer(o -> o.jwt(Customizer.withDefaults()))
        .build();
}

// URI construction
URI uri = UriComponentsBuilder.fromUriString("https://api.example.com")
    .pathSegment("users", userId, "orders")
    .queryParam("status", "active")
    .queryParam("page", page)
    .build()
    .toUri();
```

**With Lombok (practical shorthand):**

```java
@Builder
@Value // makes it immutable
public class OrderRequest {
    String customerId;
    List<String> productIds;
    String deliveryAddress;
    @Builder.Default
    Currency currency = Currency.getInstance("INR");
}

// Usage
var order = OrderRequest.builder()
    .customerId("C-123")
    .productIds(List.of("P-1", "P-2"))
    .deliveryAddress("Bengaluru, KA")
    .build();
```

### When it is the Right Choice

- The object has more than 4-5 fields, especially when most are optional.
- Construction requires validation that should happen at `build()` time, not scattered across setters.
- The object should be immutable once built.
- The same base builder needs to produce variants (test fixtures, request templates).
- Readable, self-documenting construction call sites matter.

### When it is the Wrong Choice

- The object has 2-3 required fields and nothing optional — a constructor or record is simpler.
- The object is mutable anyway (an entity managed by JPA, for example) — builders add ceremony without the immutability payoff.
- You are building a builder "because we might add fields later" — add it when the parameter list actually becomes unwieldy.

### What You Achieve When Used Well

- **Telescoping constructor problem eliminated** — no more `new Order(id, null, null, "active", null, 1)`.
- Immutable objects with clear, self-validating construction.
- Test fixtures that are readable and easy to vary: `baseRequest.toBuilder().retries(5).build()`.
- Framework fluent APIs (Spring Security, WebClient) that non-experts can configure without reading source.

### Disasters When Used Wrong

- **Mutable builder, mutable product**: if the builder holds mutable state and the caller retains the builder reference, reusing it produces aliased objects sharing the same internal collections — subtle data corruption.
- **Validation skipped**: building without required fields produces a silently broken object. Always validate at `build()`, not at field access time.
- **Builder over records**: applying `@Builder` to a 2-field record adds Lombok, a dependency, and generated code noise — where `new Point(x, y)` was three tokens and perfectly clear.
- **Too-flexible builders**: a builder that accepts any combination of fields where many combinations are logically invalid. A `build()` call that can silently produce nonsense is worse than a constructor that fails fast.

---

## 4. Prototype

### What it is

Creates new objects by copying (cloning) an existing object — the prototype. Instead of constructing from scratch, you clone and then customise.

### Applicability

| Context | Relevance | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Java 21 standalone | **Low** | `Cloneable` / `clone()` is widely considered broken. Modern Java uses copy constructors or records. Concept survives; canonical implementation does not. |
| Java 21 + Spring Boot | **Low** | `@Scope("prototype")` is named after it, but you rarely implement cloning. Spring manages the "fresh instance" concern. |

### Practical Examples

**What NOT to do — the broken `Cloneable` approach:**

```java
// Avoid — Cloneable is a marker interface with no clone() method,
// clone() is protected on Object, deep-copy semantics are undefined,
// and it bypasses constructors entirely.
public class Config implements Cloneable {
    private List<String> endpoints; // mutable — clone() will shallow-copy this!

    @Override
    public Config clone() {
        try { return (Config) super.clone(); } // endpoints list is shared — BUG
        catch (CloneNotSupportedException e) { throw new AssertionError(); }
    }
}
```

**Java 21 — copy constructor (the correct modern form):**

```java
public final class RequestTemplate {
    private final String baseUrl;
    private final Map<String, String> headers;
    private final Duration timeout;
    private final int retries;

    // Primary constructor
    public RequestTemplate(String baseUrl, Map<String, String> headers,
                           Duration timeout, int retries) {
        this.baseUrl  = baseUrl;
        this.headers  = Map.copyOf(headers); // defensive copy
        this.timeout  = timeout;
        this.retries  = retries;
    }

    // Copy constructor — the modern Prototype
    public RequestTemplate(RequestTemplate source) {
        this(source.baseUrl, source.headers, source.timeout, source.retries);
    }

    // Wither methods for one-field variations
    public RequestTemplate withTimeout(Duration newTimeout) {
        return new RequestTemplate(baseUrl, headers, newTimeout, retries);
    }
    public RequestTemplate withRetries(int newRetries) {
        return new RequestTemplate(baseUrl, headers, timeout, newRetries);
    }
}

// Usage — prototype + customise
var base = new RequestTemplate("https://api.example.com",
                               Map.of("Accept", "application/json"),
                               Duration.ofSeconds(10), 2);

var fastProbe = base.withTimeout(Duration.ofSeconds(1)).withRetries(0);
var robustCall = base.withRetries(5);
```

**Java 21 records — prototype is nearly free:**

```java
record ServiceConfig(String host, int port, boolean tls, Duration timeout) {}

var prod = new ServiceConfig("api.example.com", 443, true, Duration.ofSeconds(10));

// "Clone and customise" — idiomatic record approach
var dev  = new ServiceConfig(prod.host(), 8080, false, prod.timeout());
// Or with Lombok @With on a @Value class for named copies
```

**Spring Boot — `@Scope("prototype")` in practice:**

```java
// A stateful component — one fresh instance per injection point
@Component
@Scope("prototype")
public class ReportGenerator {
    private final List<String> warnings = new ArrayList<>(); // mutable, must not be shared

    public void addWarning(String w) { warnings.add(w); }
    public List<String> getWarnings() { return List.copyOf(warnings); }
    public byte[] generate(ReportData data) { /* ... */ }
}

// Injection — must use ObjectProvider or ApplicationContext, NOT @Autowired directly
// (direct @Autowired would inject the same instance despite prototype scope)
@Service
public class ReportService {
    private final ObjectProvider<ReportGenerator> generatorProvider;

    public ReportService(ObjectProvider<ReportGenerator> p) {
        this.generatorProvider = p;
    }

    public byte[] createReport(ReportData data) {
        ReportGenerator generator = generatorProvider.getObject(); // fresh instance
        // use generator ...
        return generator.generate(data);
    }
}
```

### When it is the Right Choice

- You need many similar objects that differ in only a few fields (request templates, test fixtures, configuration variants).
- Object construction is expensive (e.g., parsing a large schema) and you want to clone a pre-built instance instead.
- In Spring: a bean is stateful and must not be shared across call sites (`@Scope("prototype")`).

### When it is the Wrong Choice

- Objects are immutable and cheap to construct — just call the constructor.
- You reach for `Cloneable` and `clone()` — this is always the wrong choice in modern Java.
- The objects hold references to resources (connections, file handles) that should not be shared or duplicated.
- In Spring: injecting a prototype-scoped bean with plain `@Autowired` into a singleton — the prototype is only instantiated once, defeating the entire purpose.

### What You Achieve When Used Well

- Cheap creation of object variations without rebuilding from scratch.
- Clear "template + customise" pattern — a base prototype encodes shared configuration, variants override specifics.
- In Spring, stateful processing components that are safe to use per-request.

### Disasters When Used Wrong

- **Shallow copy of mutable collections**: cloning an object whose fields are mutable `List` or `Map` shares those collections between the original and the clone. Mutations in one appear in the other — a silent, hard-to-diagnose bug.
- **Bypassed invariants**: `clone()` skips constructors, so validation logic and invariant checks in the constructor never run on cloned objects. You can clone an object into an invalid state.
- **Singleton-prototype injection trap in Spring**: a singleton service holds a direct `@Autowired` reference to a prototype bean. The prototype is resolved exactly once at startup and cached in the singleton — every call uses the same stateful instance, producing shared mutable state across threads.

---

## 5. Singleton

### What it is

Ensures a class has only one instance and provides a global point of access to it.

### Applicability

| Context | Relevance | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Java 21 standalone | **Low (hand-rolled)** | Hand-rolled singleton is a near-universal anti-pattern in application code. Legitimate only for true constants or stateless utilities. |
| Java 21 + Spring Boot | **High (container-managed)** | Spring's default bean scope is singleton. You get it for free with every `@Component`. Never hand-roll it. |

### Practical Examples

**What NOT to do — the classic hand-rolled singleton:**

```java
// Avoid in application code
public class ConfigManager {
    private static volatile ConfigManager instance; // double-checked locking
    private final Map<String, String> props;

    private ConfigManager() {
        props = loadFromDisk(); // hard-coded initialization
    }

    public static ConfigManager getInstance() {
        if (instance == null) {
            synchronized (ConfigManager.class) {
                if (instance == null) instance = new ConfigManager();
            }
        }
        return instance;
    }
}
// Problems: untestable (can't inject a mock), global mutable state,
// initialization order undefined, breaks in OSGi / app-server environments.
```

**The only acceptable hand-rolled form in Java — enum singleton:**

```java
// For true constants / stateless utilities only
public enum MathConstants {
    INSTANCE;
    public double goldenRatio() { return (1 + Math.sqrt(5)) / 2; }
    public double euler()       { return Math.E; }
}
// Thread-safe, serialization-safe, but still untestable — use sparingly.
```

**Java 21 + Spring Boot — the correct modern approach:**

```java
// Just annotate it. Spring manages exactly one instance per ApplicationContext.
@Service
public class PaymentGateway {
    private final HttpClient client;
    private final PaymentConfig config;

    // Constructor injection — dependencies are explicit, testable
    public PaymentGateway(HttpClient client, PaymentConfig config) {
        this.client = client;
        this.config = config;
    }

    public PaymentResult charge(PaymentRequest req) { /* ... */ }
}

// In a test — replace the singleton with a mock, zero friction
@SpringBootTest
class PaymentGatewayTest {
    @MockBean PaymentGateway gateway; // Spring replaces the singleton for this test

    @Test
    void chargeDeclined() {
        when(gateway.charge(any())).thenReturn(PaymentResult.declined("Insufficient funds"));
        // ...
    }
}
```

**Sharing truly stateless utilities — prefer a static utility class over singleton:**

```java
// Don't make a singleton for a stateless helper — just use static methods
public final class JsonUtils {
    private static final ObjectMapper MAPPER = new ObjectMapper()
        .registerModule(new JavaTimeModule());

    private JsonUtils() {} // prevent instantiation

    public static String toJson(Object obj) throws JsonProcessingException {
        return MAPPER.writeValueAsString(obj);
    }
    public static <T> T fromJson(String json, Class<T> type) throws JsonProcessingException {
        return MAPPER.readValue(json, type);
    }
}
```

### When it is the Right Choice

- In Spring Boot: always use Spring's container-managed singleton — any `@Component`, `@Service`, `@Repository`, `@Controller` bean.
- For a truly global, stateless, immutable constant registry that needs no injection (an enum is the cleanest form).
- A shared, thread-safe resource whose initialization is expensive and must happen exactly once (connection pool, `ObjectMapper`, compiled regex) — managed by Spring or passed in via constructor injection.

### When it is the Wrong Choice

- When you hand-roll it with `static getInstance()` in application code — always wrong if you care about testing.
- When the "singleton" holds mutable state that varies per request or per user (e.g., a shopping cart, a session context) — this causes data leakage between users.
- When you choose singleton scope for a Spring bean that is stateful and not thread-safe — the single instance is shared across all concurrent requests.

### What You Achieve When Used Well

- **One authoritative instance**: connection pools, caches, configuration — there is exactly one and everyone shares it correctly.
- **Performance**: expensive initialization happens once; subsequent uses are free.
- **Consistency**: all callers see the same shared state, which is desirable for caches, rate limiters, and counters.
- **In Spring**: all of the above, plus the bean is injectable, mockable, and lifecycle-managed.

### Disasters When Used Wrong

- **Global mutable state**: a hand-rolled singleton with setters is a shared mutable variable visible to every thread in the JVM. Race conditions are guaranteed under concurrency and extremely hard to reproduce in tests.
- **Testing nightmare**: every test that calls `ConfigManager.getInstance()` gets the same instance. Tests cannot be isolated. State leaks from one test into the next. CI pipelines fail intermittently and unpredictably.
- **Classloader issues**: in application servers and OSGi containers, a static singleton may be instantiated multiple times (once per classloader), silently breaking the one-instance guarantee while `getInstance()` still returns "the" instance — which is now one of several.
- **Thread-unsafe Spring singleton**: a Spring service that caches user-specific data in an instance field is a singleton. Concurrent requests corrupt each other's data with no exception thrown — just wrong results returned to wrong users.

```java
// Classic Spring singleton disaster
@Service
public class SearchService {
    // WRONG: instance field on a singleton = shared mutable state across all threads
    private List<String> lastResults = new ArrayList<>();

    public List<String> search(String query) {
        lastResults.clear();           // Thread A clears
        lastResults.addAll(doSearch(query)); // Thread B reads a half-populated list
        return lastResults;
    }
}
// Fix: make lastResults a local variable inside the method.
```

---

## 6. Quick Reference Matrix

| Pattern | Java 21 alone | + Spring Boot | Modern form | Never do |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Factory Method** | Moderate — sealed + switch often sufficient | High — every `@Bean` is one | `switch` expression on sealed types; `@Bean` methods | Giant `if-else` factory that changes on every new type |
| **Abstract Factory** | Moderate — needed for true families | Moderate — consumed via auto-config | `@Configuration` + `@Profile` / `@ConditionalOn*` | Building a full factory hierarchy for a single concrete family |
| **Builder** | High — records handle tiny objects; builder handles everything else | High — ubiquitous in both framework and application code | `@Builder` (Lombok) or hand-rolled inner `Builder` class | Builder over a 2-field object; mutable builder sharing internal collections |
| **Prototype** | Low — `Cloneable` is dead | Low — `@Scope("prototype")` handles it | Copy constructors; wither methods; `@Scope("prototype")` | `Cloneable` + `clone()`; `@Autowired` prototype into singleton |
| **Singleton** | Low (hand-rolled) | High (container-managed) | `@Component` / `@Service` / `@Repository` | `static getInstance()` in application code; mutable instance fields on singleton beans |

---

*Generated for Java SE 21 and Spring Boot 3.x. Pattern relevance will shift further as Project Valhalla (value types) and Amber (pattern matching enhancements) mature in Java 25+.*